    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Smith-Magenis Syndrome (SMS) is a developmental disorder characterized by sleep disturbances, craniofacial abnormalities and moderate learning disability. Humans and mouse models with a deletion of Retinoic acid inducible 1 (RAI1) exhibit all aforementioned characteristics of SMS. However, the mechanisms through which RAI1 deletion leads to cognitive deficiencies is unknown. We’re interested in using mouse models with RAI1 heterozygous deletion to establish a link between sleep disturbances and cognitive deficiencies in SMS. In order to do so, we must determine if RAI1 +/- mice accurately model the cognitive and circadian phenotype of SMS patients. We found that working memory and potentially sleep quality are impaired in RAI1 +/- mice, but declarative memory is intact, suggesting dosage dependent effect of Rai1 on different cognitive processes. Moreover, one third of RAI1 deletion mice experienced seizures during sleep, suggesting that sleep seizures may be a common, underreported feature of SMS. Our results suggest that RAI1 +/- mice are a potential model of examining working memory and circadian problems in SMS, and that the latter symptom could be a cause for the former. With our model, we can discover new avenues for managing SMS, specifically the rescue of working memory through rescue of the circadian rhythm through existing sleep-inducing drugs such as trazadone.",
